Write a reaction equation that preserves atoms and charge (@gc-equations: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @general-chemistry, #source "../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94", #source "../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ", #general-chemistry "../../../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/practice/Mass-and-component-balances.lingua#r_163GVDJGRJ1DD4JT2398GM371H") { r_4MBY18QY4B7YZY9VFFZNW489C4
**Read:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapter 4’s equations and reaction classification; [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), chemical equations and stoichiometry.

A balanced chemical equation relates amounts of specified reactants and products. It conserves each element and total charge for an ordinary chemical transformation. The coefficients give stoichiometric ratios, not a promise of complete conversion, a rate law, or the sequence of elementary events. Include phases and the chemical description relevant to the intended use.

Balance by changing coefficients rather than species identities. For N₂ + 3 H₂ → 2 NH₃, one mole of nitrogen and three moles of hydrogen correspond to two moles of ammonia at the stated reaction extent. The total molecular amount changes while atoms are conserved; total gas volume need not remain unchanged at a common temperature and pressure.

Matrix bookkeeping provides a deeper interpretation. A composition matrix contains element counts and charges for species; a stoichiometric vector satisfies its conservation constraints. Several balanced reaction vectors may describe independent changes among the same set of species. A balanced relation alone does not select the actual physical process.

**Work:** balance complete molecular and ionic equations from the textbook, checking each element and charge independently. For one equation write the composition table and show that the stoichiometric changes lie in its null space. Begin with ordinary algebra; use later linear-algebra preparation if the matrix treatment is new.

Follow [Mass and component balances — account for every stream](../../../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/practice/Mass-and-component-balances.lingua#r_163GVDJGRJ1DD4JT2398GM371H) for shared control-volume and stream accounting. Its general-chemistry continuation returns to reaction extent, where conserved elemental amounts coexist with changing chemical-species amounts. This reuses the existing generic balance Record rather than teaching a second conflicting version of conservation.

